The attached image displays immunofluorescence microscopy of skeletal muscle 24 hours after intramuscular injection, illustrating Nano-11's role in inducing inflammation and antigen uptake. The top row, corresponding to Nano-11 formulated with ovalbumin (OVA), shows a marked accumulation of Mac-2+ monocytes/macrophages, MHCII+ cells, and Ly-6G+ neutrophils. In contrast, the bottom row, representing soluble OVA injection, shows few inflammatory cells. Intracellular Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 is clearly visible within Mac-2+ and MHCII+ cells, indicating efficient phagocytosis in vivo. This demonstrates Nano-11's ability to enhance immune cell recruitment and facilitate antigen delivery at the injection site.